## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to reason with Kleene's three-valued logic and implement a lightweight backtracking propagator over a small, non-binary constraint set.

## Key Concepts
- **Kleene 3-valued semantics**: Values ordered T > U > F; negation of U remains U
- **Implication equivalence**: a→b = ¬a ∨ b
- **Constraint propagation**: Propagating partial information through implications
- **Goal**: Produce a non-F assignment that still utilizes the unknown value U

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v) {
  if (v === 'T') return 'F';
  if (v === 'F') return 'T';
  return 'U';
}

function or(a, b) {
  if (a === 'T' || b === 'T') return 'T';
  if (a === 'F' && b === 'F') return 'F';
  return 'U';
}

function and(a, b) {
  if (a === 'F' || b === 'F') return 'F';
  if (a === 'T' && b === 'T') return 'T';
  return 'U';
}

function evalRule(rule, vars) {
  const [a, b] = rule.map(v => vars[v]);
  return or(not(a), b);
}

function propagate(vars, rules) {
  for (const rule of rules) {
    const result = evalRule(rule, vars);
    if (result === 'F') return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of temporal, multi-step constraints in a 3-valued logic setting. Requires constructing a concrete model and outlining a backtracking propagator that preserves non-F constraints while allowing Unknowns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued semantics for implication
- Temporal constraint propagation across time steps
- Unit/branching propagation in backtracking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: evaluateImp(A,B) returns Kleene(A,B)
function evaluateImp(A,B){ // A,B in {T,F,U}
  // ¬A ∨ B with Kleene ordering T>U>F
  const not = {T:'F', F:'T', U:'U'}[A];
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  // ⩒ is max by value order
  const values = [not, B];
  let best = 'F';
  for (const v of values) if (order[v] > order[best]) best = v;
  return best;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason through 3-valued logic and implement a constrained solver with backtracking—a common pattern in policy/rule engines.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic with unknown (U) support
- Implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Backtracking propagation to avoid false outcomes

## Code Example

```javascript
function kleeneImpl(a,b){
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const order = {'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
  const na = not(a);
  const vals = [na, b];
  return vals.reduce((m,x)=> order[x] > order[m] ? x : m, 'F');
}
```

